From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 03:37:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Add an embedded macOS platform for an application that
 owns NSApp

Application::run_embedded exists for a GPUI app whose run loop belongs to
someone else ("a GPUI view hosted inside a foreign native application"),
but on macOS the platform cannot be hosted: MacPlatform::run makes
GPUIApplication the shared application, installs its own delegate, writes
the platform pointer into both, sets the activation policy and blocks in
[NSApp run]. A native application that owns NSApp (its class, delegate,
menus and run loop) and shows some windows with GPUI cannot use it.

MacPlatform::new_embedded (gpui_platform::embedded_application) makes a
platform, in an Rc since running it hands its address to Objective-C,
whose run never touches NSApp: not its class, delegate, ivars, activation
policy or run loop. It calls the launch callback and returns, so
run_embedded hands the host an ApplicationHandle; the host starts it once
it has finished launching, and only once. GPUI's notifications (keyboard
layout, thermal state, sleep and wake) go to an observer, a
GPUIApplicationDelegate that is never NSApp's delegate, which carries the
platform ivar; the platform removes it from both notification centres,
clears its ivar and releases it when dropped. The notifications are
subscribed by the code that did it in applicationDidFinishLaunching:, now
a function both use.

What belongs to the application is the host's: set_menus only keeps the
menus for get_menus, set_dock_menu does nothing, system notifications do
nothing (GPUI never takes over the UNUserNotificationCenter's delegate or
categories), and the delegate's handlers (should-quit, quit, reopen, open
URLs, the app menu's) are not called; the host calls what it needs, such
as App::shutdown when it terminates. What acts on the application (quit,
restart, activate, hide, the appearance, simple fullscreen's presentation
options) acts on the host's; new_embedded's documentation lists it.
Windows, events, text input, cursors and dispatching to the main thread
already use only public NSApplication API, whoever created NSApp, and need
no change.

Since an embedded platform can go away before the process does, the
thermal, sleep and wake handlers no longer queue the platform's address
for the main queue: they queue the observer, retained, and the queued
work looks the platform up on the main thread (thermal notifications
arrive on any thread) and does nothing if it has gone. Every delegate
handler now does nothing when there is no platform, where it asserted
(and so aborted the process, a panic in an extern "C" function).

For an application GPUI runs, the other changes: run no longer writes the
platform ivar into an NSApp that is not a GPUIApplication
(sharedApplication returns an NSApp created before it, which has no such
ivar, so run used to panic there; only the delegate's ivar is ever read);
set_menus, set_dock_menu, ensure_system_power_observers and the system
notification functions check `embedded` first; MacPlatform has a Drop,
which does nothing unless it was embedded; and the deferred handlers
retain and release the delegate around the queued work.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
